latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Dividing Lines:\n   a. Scan the grid horizontally and vertically to find all rows and columns that consist entirely of colors 6 or 2.\n   b. Store the indices of these dividing lines.\n\n2. Define Sections:\n   a. Use the dividing line indices to create a list of rectangular sections.\n   b. Each section is defined by its top, left, bottom, and right coordinates.\n\n3. Process Each Section:\n   For each section:\n   a. Calculate the middle row of the section: middle = (top + bottom) // 2\n   b. For each column in the section (from left to right):\n      i. Copy the pattern from the top half (top to middle, inclusive)\n      ii. Reflect this pattern to the bottom half:\n         - For row in range(top, middle + 1):\n           mirror_row = bottom - (row - top)\n           section[mirror_row][column] = section[row][column]\n\n4. Preserve Dividing Lines:\n   a. After processing all sections, restore all cells along the dividing lines to their original values (6 or 2).\n\n5. Handle Empty Sections:\n   a. If a section contains only 0s (excluding dividing lines), leave it unchanged.\n\n6. Construct the Output Grid:\n   a. Create a new grid with the same dimensions as the input.\n   b. Fill in the new grid with the processed section data.\n   c. Ensure all dividing lines are correctly placed in the new grid.\n\n7. Validate the Output:\n   a. Check that all dividing lines are preserved.\n   b. Verify that each section's top half matches the input and the bottom half is a mirror of the top half.\n   c. Confirm that empty sections remain empty.\n\n8. Return the Transformed Grid:\n   a. Return the new grid as the solution.\n\nImplementation Notes:\n- Use the ColoredGrid class methods like get_dimensions(), deep_copy(), and is_valid to handle grid operations.\n- Implement helper functions for tasks like identifying dividing lines, defining sections, and mirroring patterns within a section.\n- Ensure that the mirroring process doesn't modify cells that are part of dividing lines.\n- When mirroring, only copy non-zero, non-divider colors to preserve the original pattern structure.\n\nThis plan should correctly handle all the cases presented in the challenge, including the examples and test cases. It maintains the integrity of each section, correctly mirrors patterns, preserves dividing lines, and handles empty sections appropriately."
